\section{Model definitions and surrogate profiles}
\markboth{Model definitions}{Model definitions}
\field{Virtual shares and ordinal restrictions}
For a positive weighted total $T_o=\sum_{r=1}^s u_r y_{ro}$, define
\[
w_{or}=\frac{u_r y_{ro}}{T_o},\qquad \sum_{r=1}^s w_{or}=1,\qquad w_{or}\geq 0.
\]
An input-side definition replaces $u_r y_{ro}$ with $v_i x_{io}$. Rank labels are sorted in ascending order, and $[h]$ denotes the indicator at position $h$. Let $\tau_{o[h]}$ be the normalized surrogate share. Weak preferences ($\gamma=0$) impose the ordering and bottom floor. The unified moderate model is
\begin{align*}
w_{o[h]}-w_{o[h+1]} &\geq \gamma(\tau_{o[h]}-\tau_{o[h+1]}),\quad h=1,\ldots,s-1,\\
w_{o[s]} &\geq \tau_{o[s]},\qquad 0\leq \gamma\leq 1.
\end{align*}
For any $\gamma$ the inequalities are linear after multiplying by the positive total. At $\gamma=1$, recursive substitution implies $w_{o[h]}\geq\tau_{o[h]}$ for every $h$. Since both vectors sum to one, all inequalities are equalities. The strong model therefore directly sets $w_{or}=\tau_{or}$. When a contribution total vanishes, these share identities are undefined; inspect returned shares rather than treating the homogeneous inequalities as proof of strict compliance.
\field{Pooled positional mass for ties}
If a tied block $C$ occupies positions $a,\ldots,b$, set
\[
\widetilde\tau_{or}=\frac{1}{b-a+1}\sum_{h=a}^{b}\tau_h,\qquad r\in C.
\]
All members have equal virtual shares. Apply the adjacent-gap restrictions between blocks, not within them. Averaging a partition of the sorted vector preserves its total mass and monotonicity. For four criteria, ROC gives $(25,13,7,3)/48$; if the top two criteria tie, the adjusted vector is $(19,19,7,3)/48$. The old worst-position substitution can duplicate or omit positional masses and is not used.
\field{Surrogate rules}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{21mm}p{54mm}p{75mm}@{}}
\toprule
Rule & Base share at rank $h$ & Interpretation \\
\midrule
ROC & $s^{-1}\sum_{k=h}^s k^{-1}$ & Rank-order centroid. \\
RS & $2(s+1-h)/[s(s+1)]$ & Linear decline across positions. \\
RR & $h^{-1}/\sum_{k=1}^s k^{-1}$ & Reciprocal rank profile. \\
RE & $h^{-\alpha}/\sum_{k=1}^s k^{-\alpha}$ & Tunable inverse-power concentration. \\
EW & $1/s$ & Equal shares; no differentiation. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\par The five profiles encode different degrees of preference concentration. Compare the top-to-bottom ratios and elicit acceptable trade-offs before selecting a strict surrogate. In PORTO, RE specifically denotes the inverse-power form inherited from the project, not every possible rank-exponent rule used in MCDA.
\field{Example surrogate weights for four positions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}\toprule Rule & Rank 1 & Rank 2 & Rank 3 & Rank 4 \\ \midrule
RS & 0.4000 & 0.3000 & 0.2000 & 0.1000 \\
ROC & 0.5208 & 0.2708 & 0.1458 & 0.0625 \\
RR & 0.4800 & 0.2400 & 0.1600 & 0.1200 \\
RE ($\alpha=2$) & 0.7024 & 0.1756 & 0.0780 & 0.0439 \\
EW & 0.2500 & 0.2500 & 0.2500 & 0.2500 \\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}
\par Values rounded for display. The implementation uses full precision.
\field{Radial formulation}
For all reference observations $j$, the multiplier restrictions are
\[
u^\top y_j-v^\top x_j-\omega\leq 0,\quad u,v\geq 0.
\]
The intercept is zero in CRS and unrestricted in VRS. With \texttt{ORIENTATION="out"}, maximize $u^\top y_o-\omega$ with $v^\top x_o=1$. With \texttt{ORIENTATION="in"}, minimize $v^\top x_o+\omega$ with $u^\top y_o=1$ and return its reciprocal. In unit-input CRS BoD the first case becomes $\max u^\top y_o$ subject to $u^\top y_j\leq1$. The two historical argument labels describe the objective, not the conventional envelopment orientation names. The intercept is excluded from the normalized virtual contributions.
\field{Directional formulation}
Let $y$ be desirable, $b$ undesirable, and $g_y,g_b\geq0$ the magnitudes of expansion and contraction. PORTO solves
\begin{align*}
\min_{u,p,\omega}\quad &\beta_o=-u^\top y_o+p^\top b_o+\omega\\
\text{s.t.}\quad &-u^\top y_j+p^\top b_j+\omega\geq0\quad\forall j,\\
&u^\top g_{y,o}+p^\top g_{b,o}=1,\qquad u,p\geq0.
\end{align*}
Under VRS $\omega$ is free; under CRS $\omega=0$. For the data direction $(g_y,-g_b)=(y_o,-b_o)$, the directional shares are $u_r y_{ro}$ and $p_k b_{ko}$. For custom directions, they are $u_r g_{y,r}$ and $p_k g_{b,k}$. Their combined sum is one. Preference rules are applied jointly across desirable and undesirable indicators. The package reports $CI_o=1/(1+\beta_o)$; a score of one corresponds to a zero support gap. This convention is not interchangeable with other index transformations. With restrictions the minimized support gap may differ from a physically attainable radial or directional target.
\field{Numerical considerations}
A nonzero solver status is not a valid score. Never replace such estimates with one or zero. Use nonnegative finite observations, substantively meaningful positive directions for ranked indicators, and careful row/column alignment. Weak/strict comparisons should use identical data, direction and surrogate scheme. An optimum multiplier vector may be nonunique. With DMU-specific virtual restrictions, comparisons are conditional on each DMU's preferences; neither the resulting score nor its decrease is causal evidence.

\topic{dea.order}{Ordinal Data Envelopment Analysis and Benefit-of-the-Doubt without Ties}
\field{Description}
Estimates radial DEA multiplier scores or unit-input/unit-output BoD scores for rankings without ties.
\field{Usage}
\begin{verbatim}
dea.order(X = NULL, Y = NULL, OX = NULL, OY = NULL,
                      RTS = "crs", ORIENTATION = "out",
                      SURROGATE = "ROC", Weak = TRUE,
                      ALPHA = 2, AUTO_CRS_BOD = TRUE, Gamma = NULL)
\end{verbatim}
\field{Arguments}
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
\texttt{X} & Numeric n by m input matrix or data frame (DMUs in rows). Nonnegative, finite values. NULL creates a single column of ones if Y is given. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Y} & Numeric n by s desirable-output matrix or data frame. NULL creates a single column of ones if X is given. At least one of X or Y is required. \\[2pt]
\texttt{OX} & Numeric ordinal rank matrix with the same dimensions and order as X. Smaller positive labels mean higher importance. NULL or an entire all-zero matrix disables input restrictions; partial zero ranks are invalid. \\[2pt]
\texttt{OY} & Numeric ordinal rank matrix with the same dimensions and order as Y. Smaller positive labels mean higher importance. NULL or an entire all-zero matrix disables output restrictions; partial zero ranks are invalid. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS} & Returns to scale: "crs" or "vrs" (case-insensitive). Radial models default to CRS; directional models default to VRS. VRS uses a free intercept, represented as the difference of two nonnegative variables. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ORIENTATION} & "out" (default) maximizes weighted output with weighted input normalized to one; "in" minimizes weighted input with weighted output normalized to one and returns the reciprocal objective. These are historical interface labels, not the conventional names of radial DEA orientations: the first is the input-normalized multiplier form, the second the output-normalized form. \\[2pt]
\texttt{SURROGATE} & Surrogate method: "ROC" (rank-order centroid, default), "RS" (rank sum), "RR" (rank reciprocal), "RE" (inverse-power rank exponent), or "EW" (equal weights). Methods are case-insensitive. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Weak} & Logical scalar. TRUE (default) gives weak/benevolent preferences; FALSE imposes strong/strict exact surrogate shares. With TRUE, Gamma may specify the moderate family. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ALPHA} & Positive finite numeric scalar; exponent for RE, default 2. RE uses r\textasciicircum{}(-ALPHA) normalized across the rank positions. Other schemes do not use the exponent. \\[2pt]
\texttt{AUTO\_CRS\_BOD} & Logical scalar (default TRUE). If a unit input or output was automatically created and VRS requested, warn and use CRS. FALSE retains the requested VRS model; identical constant inputs or outputs can cause degeneracy. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Gamma} & NULL (default) selects 0 when Weak=TRUE and 1 when Weak=FALSE. Supply a value in [0,1] with Weak=TRUE for the nested preference continuum. 0 is weak, 1 is strict, and intermediate values are moderate. Weak=FALSE requires Gamma=NULL or Gamma=1. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Details}
Let x\_j and y\_j denote the input/output vectors of DMU j and v and u the corresponding nonnegative multipliers. The common supporting inequalities are u'y\_j - v'x\_j - omega \textless{}= 0 for all j. Under CRS omega=0; under VRS omega is free. ORIENTATION="out" maximizes u'y\_o - omega subject to v'x\_o=1. ORIENTATION="in" minimizes v'x\_o + omega subject to u'y\_o=1; the returned score is the reciprocal of that objective. These labels are retained for compatibility with the original routines. The CRS scores lie between zero and one for feasible nonnegative data with valid normalization.

For ordinary BoD supply Y only: X is a single unit input and the CRS constraints become u'y\_j \textless{}= 1. For an all-undesirable radial assessment, supply X only and use ORIENTATION="in" (or the other CRS normalization, which gives the same efficiency under homogeneous restrictions). In conventional DEA, input and output shares are separately normalized; this does not impose a joint ranking across inputs and outputs. Use the directional functions for a joint ranking of good and bad indicators.

Ordinal share constraints are linearized by multiplying by the corresponding contribution total. For example, a moderate output restriction is u\_a y\_ao - u\_b y\_bo \textgreater{}= Gamma (tau\_a-tau\_b) sum\_r u\_r y\_ro. A strict constraint is u\_r y\_ro = tau\_r sum\_q u\_q y\_qo. The VRS intercept is not part of the virtual-share denominator.

Any repeated positive rank within a ranked row is rejected. Use the corresponding ties function for indifference. All-zero rank matrices are the documented exception and mean no restrictions.
See Section 2 for the exact surrogate rules and weak, moderate and strict preference constraints.
\field{Value}
A list containing the following elements:
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
\texttt{efficiency} & Numeric vector of higher-is-better scores, one per DMU. The score equals the objective for ORIENTATION="out" and its reciprocal for "in"; NA when the solver fails or the reciprocal is undefined. \\[2pt]
\texttt{objective\_raw} & Raw optimized LP objective, before the reciprocal transformation, where applicable. \\[2pt]
\texttt{weights\_input}, \texttt{weights\_output} & Matrices of optimal nonnegative DEA multipliers, one row per DMU; an optimum need not be unique. \\[2pt]
\texttt{virtual\_input}, \texttt{virtual\_output} & Matrices of normalized virtual contributions for each side separately, equal to the multiplier times the observed value divided by the corresponding total. A zero total is undefined and reported as NA. \\[2pt]
\texttt{surrogate\_input}, \texttt{surrogate\_output} & Rank-aligned surrogate vectors for each side (all zeros on an unrestricted side). An active row sums to one. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ordinal\_positions\_input}, \texttt{ordinal\_positions\_output} & Rank labels converted to occupied positions; for ties, the average occupied position is returned. \\[2pt]
\texttt{potential\_improvement} & Matrix of proportional output expansion amounts, (1/efficiency - 1) times Y, only for CRS and ORIENTATION="out" with positive scores. Unused columns and other cases are NA. With weight restrictions this is an algebraic indicator, not a guaranteed feasible physical projection. \\[2pt]
\texttt{solver\_status} & Integer status returned by lpSolve, 0 for optimal. Nonzero statuses have missing estimates; the function also issues a warning. \\[2pt]
\texttt{preference}, \texttt{Weak}, \texttt{Gamma} & Effective preference name, logical flag (TRUE for weak or moderate), and intensity value. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS}, \texttt{RTS\_requested}, \texttt{ORIENTATION}, \texttt{SURROGATE}, \texttt{ALPHA} & Effective/requested returns to scale and other model settings. \\[2pt]
\texttt{auto\_unit\_input}, \texttt{auto\_unit\_output} & Flags indicating whether a single artificial unit input/output was generated. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Note}
For ranked sides, strictly positive observed values are recommended. A zero observation can force zero virtual contributions; if the entire weighted total is zero, the linearized share restrictions are vacuous and the shares are undefined. Inspect the virtual shares and status before interpreting strict preferences. No artificial epsilon is inserted. The constant-input VRS radial model can return uninformative scores of one. Restrictions depend on the evaluated DMU, so the effective feasible multiplier sets differ across DMUs. Do not interpret a decrease in score as a causal policy effect. Multipliers and rankings may not be unique; inspect alternative optima if needed.
