dea.order.ties(Y = Y, OY = OY, Gamma = 0.5)$efficiency
\end{verbatim}

\topic{dir.dea.order}{Directional Ordinal Benefit-of-the-Doubt without Ties}
\field{Description}
Estimates directional composite indicators with desirable and undesirable dimensions for rankings without ties.
\field{Usage}
\begin{verbatim}
dir.dea.order(Y = NULL, B = NULL, OY = NULL, OB = NULL,
                          RTS = "vrs", SURROGATE = "ROC", Weak = TRUE,
                          ALPHA = 2, GY = NULL, GB = NULL, Gamma = NULL)
\end{verbatim}
\field{Arguments}
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
\texttt{Y} & Numeric n by s\_g matrix of desirable indicators, larger is better. NULL permits an all-undesirable assessment. Nonnegative finite values, DMUs in rows. \\[2pt]
\texttt{B} & Numeric n by s\_b matrix of undesirable indicators, smaller is better. NULL permits an all-desirable assessment. At least one of Y and B must be supplied; row counts must agree. \\[2pt]
\texttt{OY} & Ordinal ranks for Y, aligned by row and column. Smaller labels represent higher priority. When both Y and B exist, OY and OB must jointly encode the full ordering across both groups, not independent rank sequences. \\[2pt]
\texttt{OB} & Ordinal ranks for B, aligned by row and column. Supply positive finite ranks for every included indicator when restrictions are active. Both rank matrices NULL or jointly all zero disable restrictions; mixed zero and positive ranks are invalid. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS} & Returns to scale: "crs" or "vrs" (case-insensitive). Radial models default to CRS; directional models default to VRS. VRS uses a free intercept, represented as the difference of two nonnegative variables. \\[2pt]
\texttt{SURROGATE} & Surrogate method: "ROC" (rank-order centroid, default), "RS" (rank sum), "RR" (rank reciprocal), "RE" (inverse-power rank exponent), or "EW" (equal weights). Methods are case-insensitive. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Weak} & Logical scalar. TRUE (default) gives weak/benevolent preferences; FALSE imposes strong/strict exact surrogate shares. With TRUE, Gamma may specify the moderate family. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ALPHA} & Positive finite numeric scalar; exponent for RE, default 2. RE uses r\textasciicircum{}(-ALPHA) normalized across the rank positions. Other schemes do not use the exponent. \\[2pt]
\texttt{GY} & Nonnegative direction magnitudes for desirable indicators: vector of length ncol(Y) shared by all DMUs, or matrix of the same dimensions as Y. NULL uses each evaluated DMU's observed desirable values. \\[2pt]
\texttt{GB} & Nonnegative direction magnitudes for undesirable indicators: vector of length ncol(B), or matrix of the same dimensions as B. NULL uses each evaluated DMU's observed undesirable values. Supply positive magnitudes for contraction, not negative numbers. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Gamma} & NULL (default) selects 0 when Weak=TRUE and 1 when Weak=FALSE. Supply a value in [0,1] with Weak=TRUE for the nested preference continuum. 0 is weak, 1 is strict, and intermediate values are moderate. Weak=FALSE requires Gamma=NULL or Gamma=1. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Details}
For desirable values y\_j and undesirable values b\_j, solve a directional multiplier minimization with nonnegative multipliers u and p. The objective for unit o is -u'y\_o + p'b\_o + omega. The common supporting inequalities are -u'y\_j + p'b\_j + omega \textgreater{}= 0 for every j. Normalize u'g\_y + p'g\_b = 1, with nonnegative direction magnitudes g\_y and g\_b; the actual direction expands y and contracts b. Under VRS omega is free; under CRS omega=0. The default data direction uses g\_y=y\_o and g\_b=b\_o. At least one direction component must be positive for every DMU. Custom magnitudes change the distance scale and thus the numerical score.

Directional shares are u\_r g\_y,r and p\_k g\_b,k, whose combined sum is one by normalization. They encode the magnitude of weighted directional improvement, not signed net achievement. Apply the weak, moderate or strict rules to that combined vector. When using the data direction these are the weighted observed values. No reversal of undesirable indicators is performed. The score is 1/(1+beta), where beta is the minimized objective. This reciprocal scaling is PORTO's convention and differs from other DDF-to-index transformations; do not mix scales.

The nonnegative bad-indicator multipliers represent a monotone (free-disposal) supporting technology for the undesirable dimensions. This is not a weak-disposability equality model with unrestricted bad-output multipliers. The VRS model uses a convex reference set. Without the VRS intercept the CRS directional model uses a conical technology and may be infeasible; VRS is generally preferred for the composite-indicator setting. With ordinal restrictions, the distance is a restricted supporting gap, not necessarily a physically attainable improvement in the unmodified envelopment technology.

Any repeated positive rank within a ranked row is rejected. Use the corresponding ties function for indifference. All-zero rank matrices are the documented exception and mean no restrictions.
See Section 2 for the exact surrogate rules and weak, moderate and strict preference constraints.
\field{Value}
A list containing the following elements:
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
\texttt{efficiency}, \texttt{score} & Identical numeric vectors of higher-is-better composite scores, computed as 1/(1+beta). NA for nonoptimal solves or invalid negative distances. \\[2pt]
\texttt{beta} & Optimal directional distance (or restricted directional support gap). Smaller is better; zero indicates a supporting frontier. Tiny negative round-off is truncated to zero. \\[2pt]
\texttt{weights\_desirable}, \texttt{weights\_undesirable} & Matrices of nonnegative multipliers for Y and B, one row per DMU. \\[2pt]
\texttt{virtual\_share} & Combined directional contribution shares, in column order cbind(Y,B). With data direction these are multiplier times observed indicator. With custom directions these are multiplier times direction magnitude. Each valid row sums to one. \\[2pt]
\texttt{surrogate\_share} & Combined rank-aligned surrogate shares, in the same column order. All zeros if restrictions are disabled. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ordinal\_positions} & Converted positional ranks; tied blocks use the average occupied position. \\[2pt]
\texttt{solver\_status} & Integer lpSolve status per DMU (0 is optimal). Nonzero status yields NA estimates and a warning. \\[2pt]
\texttt{preference}, \texttt{Weak}, \texttt{Gamma} & Effective preference name, flag and intensity. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS}, \texttt{SURROGATE}, \texttt{ALPHA} & Model settings used. \\[2pt]
\texttt{direction} & Label indicating data direction or user-supplied directions. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Note}
A zero direction component cannot receive a positive share. Weak, moderate or strict constraints can therefore be infeasible when the default data direction contains zeros. Choose substantively defensible positive direction magnitudes where appropriate; no epsilon or automatic imputation is inserted. Nonnegative data and finite ranks are required. Directional restrictions are DMU-specific; comparative scores describe conditional support, not causal effects of policies. Directional targets are not returned, and the restricted gap need not correspond to a feasible physical target. Rescale values and directions together if numerical conditioning is poor.
\field{Author(s)}
Thyago Nepomuceno, Department of Statistics, Federal University of Pernambuco. Email: nepomuceno@de.ufpe.be.
\field{See Also}
\texttt{dea.order}, \texttt{dea.order.ties}, \texttt{dir.dea.order.ties}
\field{Examples}
\begin{verbatim}
Y <- matrix(c(5, 7, 6), ncol = 1)
B <- matrix(c(4, 3, 5), ncol = 1)
OY <- matrix(1, nrow = 3, ncol = 1)
OB <- matrix(2, nrow = 3, ncol = 1)
w <- dir.dea.order(Y = Y, B = B, OY = OY, OB = OB)
s <- dir.dea.order(Y = Y, B = B, OY = OY, OB = OB,
                   Weak = FALSE)
cbind(w$score, s$score)
s$beta
s$virtual_share  # 0.75 and 0.25 in every row
s$solver_status

# Unit directions instead of proportional data directions
fit <- dir.dea.order(Y = Y, B = B, OY = OY, OB = OB,
                     GY = 1, GB = 1, Gamma = 0.5)
fit$score
# Desirable-only model with a common unit direction
dir.dea.order(Y = Y, GY = 1)$beta
\end{verbatim}

\topic{dir.dea.order.ties}{Directional Ordinal Benefit-of-the-Doubt with Ties}
\field{Description}
Estimates directional composite indicators with desirable and undesirable dimensions and pooled positional treatment of tied priorities.
\field{Usage}
\begin{verbatim}
dir.dea.order.ties(Y = NULL, B = NULL, OY = NULL, OB = NULL,
                               RTS = "vrs", SURROGATE = "ROC", Weak = TRUE,
                               ALPHA = 2, TIES = "pooled",
                               GY = NULL, GB = NULL, Gamma = NULL)
\end{verbatim}
\field{Arguments}
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
