\texttt{OY} & Ordinal ranks for Y, aligned by row and column. Smaller labels represent higher priority. When both Y and B exist, OY and OB must jointly encode the full ordering across both groups, not independent rank sequences. \\[2pt]
\texttt{OB} & Ordinal ranks for B, aligned by row and column. Supply positive finite ranks for every included indicator when restrictions are active. Both rank matrices NULL or jointly all zero disable restrictions; mixed zero and positive ranks are invalid. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS} & Returns to scale: "crs" or "vrs" (case-insensitive). Radial models default to CRS; directional models default to VRS. VRS uses a free intercept, represented as the difference of two nonnegative variables. \\[2pt]
\texttt{SURROGATE} & Surrogate method: "ROC" (rank-order centroid, default), "RS" (rank sum), "RR" (rank reciprocal), "RE" (inverse-power rank exponent), or "EW" (equal weights). Methods are case-insensitive. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Weak} & Logical scalar. TRUE (default) gives weak/benevolent preferences; FALSE imposes strong/strict exact surrogate shares. With TRUE, Gamma may specify the moderate family. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ALPHA} & Positive finite numeric scalar; exponent for RE, default 2. RE uses r\textasciicircum{}(-ALPHA) normalized across the rank positions. Other schemes do not use the exponent. \\[2pt]
\texttt{TIES} & Character scalar, "pooled" only. Tied indicators share the total surrogate mass of the occupied positions equally. Legacy best, worst, average and sequential substitutions are not supported. \\[2pt]
\texttt{GY} & Nonnegative direction magnitudes for desirable indicators: vector of length ncol(Y) shared by all DMUs, or matrix of the same dimensions as Y. NULL uses each evaluated DMU's observed desirable values. \\[2pt]
\texttt{GB} & Nonnegative direction magnitudes for undesirable indicators: vector of length ncol(B), or matrix of the same dimensions as B. NULL uses each evaluated DMU's observed undesirable values. Supply positive magnitudes for contraction, not negative numbers. \\[2pt]
\texttt{Gamma} & NULL (default) selects 0 when Weak=TRUE and 1 when Weak=FALSE. Supply a value in [0,1] with Weak=TRUE for the nested preference continuum. 0 is weak, 1 is strict, and intermediate values are moderate. Weak=FALSE requires Gamma=NULL or Gamma=1. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Details}
For desirable values y\_j and undesirable values b\_j, solve a directional multiplier minimization with nonnegative multipliers u and p. The objective for unit o is -u'y\_o + p'b\_o + omega. The common supporting inequalities are -u'y\_j + p'b\_j + omega \textgreater{}= 0 for every j. Normalize u'g\_y + p'g\_b = 1, with nonnegative direction magnitudes g\_y and g\_b; the actual direction expands y and contracts b. Under VRS omega is free; under CRS omega=0. The default data direction uses g\_y=y\_o and g\_b=b\_o. At least one direction component must be positive for every DMU. Custom magnitudes change the distance scale and thus the numerical score.

Directional shares are u\_r g\_y,r and p\_k g\_b,k, whose combined sum is one by normalization. They encode the magnitude of weighted directional improvement, not signed net achievement. Apply the weak, moderate or strict rules to that combined vector. When using the data direction these are the weighted observed values. No reversal of undesirable indicators is performed. The score is 1/(1+beta), where beta is the minimized objective. This reciprocal scaling is PORTO's convention and differs from other DDF-to-index transformations; do not mix scales.

