Ties represent indifference, not missing information. If a tied block occupies consecutive positions a through b in the sorted list, each member receives the mean of all base surrogate weights at those positions. For four ROC ranks with the top two tied, the exact vector is (19/48, 19/48, 7/48, 1/16). This preserves the unit sum and ordering. Weak and moderate models impose equality within tied blocks and compare consecutive blocks. Strict models fix each member to its pooled share. The legacy worst-position rule is not equivalent.
See Section 2 for the exact surrogate rules and weak, moderate and strict preference constraints.
\field{Value}
A list containing the following elements:
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{53mm}p{106mm}@{}}
\texttt{efficiency}, \texttt{score} & Identical numeric vectors of higher-is-better composite scores, computed as 1/(1+beta). NA for nonoptimal solves or invalid negative distances. \\[2pt]
\texttt{beta} & Optimal directional distance (or restricted directional support gap). Smaller is better; zero indicates a supporting frontier. Tiny negative round-off is truncated to zero. \\[2pt]
\texttt{weights\_desirable}, \texttt{weights\_undesirable} & Matrices of nonnegative multipliers for Y and B, one row per DMU. \\[2pt]
\texttt{virtual\_share} & Combined directional contribution shares, in column order cbind(Y,B). With data direction these are multiplier times observed indicator. With custom directions these are multiplier times direction magnitude. Each valid row sums to one. \\[2pt]
\texttt{surrogate\_share} & Combined rank-aligned surrogate shares, in the same column order. All zeros if restrictions are disabled. \\[2pt]
\texttt{ordinal\_positions} & Converted positional ranks; tied blocks use the average occupied position. \\[2pt]
\texttt{solver\_status} & Integer lpSolve status per DMU (0 is optimal). Nonzero status yields NA estimates and a warning. \\[2pt]
\texttt{preference}, \texttt{Weak}, \texttt{Gamma} & Effective preference name, flag and intensity. \\[2pt]
\texttt{RTS}, \texttt{SURROGATE}, \texttt{ALPHA} & Model settings used. \\[2pt]
\texttt{direction} & Label indicating data direction or user-supplied directions. \\[2pt]
\texttt{tie\_blocks} & List(s) of integer column indices in each priority block, ordered from highest to lowest, one list per DMU. On inactive rank sets the entries are empty. \\[2pt]
\texttt{tie\_method} & Description of the pooled positional mass rule. \\[2pt]
\end{longtable}
\field{Note}
A zero direction component cannot receive a positive share. Weak, moderate or strict constraints can therefore be infeasible when the default data direction contains zeros. Choose substantively defensible positive direction magnitudes where appropriate; no epsilon or automatic imputation is inserted. Nonnegative data and finite ranks are required. Directional restrictions are DMU-specific; comparative scores describe conditional support, not causal effects of policies. Directional targets are not returned, and the restricted gap need not correspond to a feasible physical target. Rescale values and directions together if numerical conditioning is poor.
